<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ignatius of Loyola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1491">1491</a>–<a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ignatius of Loyola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ignatius of Loyola (1491–1556) was a Basque nobleman and soldier who founded the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Badly wounded defending the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> against a Franco-Navarrese army in 1521, he underwent a religious conversion during his recovery and wrote the Spiritual Exercises. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> order, approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> in 1540, became a leading force of the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, education and missions. He was canonised in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola.jpg" alt="Ignatius of Loyola" width="249" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ignatius_Loyola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
